1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,240
In 2021, a trial in Yogyakarta, Indonesia, reported

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:07,760
seventy-seven per cent fewer dengue cases where Wolbachia

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,280
mosquitoes had been released.

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,360
Yet in 2024, countries reported more dengue than

5
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:16,880
ever recorded, over fourteen million cases.

6
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:20,000
So if one method works that well, what

7
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:21,520
limits dengue control?

8
00:00:22,734 --> 00:00:25,453
Our named disease is dengue in South East

9
00:00:25,534 --> 00:00:26,013
Asia.

10
00:00:26,574 --> 00:00:29,213
Dengue is caused by a virus, spread mainly

11
00:00:29,294 --> 00:00:32,493
by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes, which breed in water

12
00:00:32,574 --> 00:00:34,253
containers around homes.

13
00:00:34,574 --> 00:00:37,133
Malaria is the other choice: it's caused by

14
00:00:37,214 --> 00:00:40,493
Plasmodium, a protozoan, spread by Anopheles.

15
00:00:41,054 --> 00:00:43,933
And the virus comes in four serotypes, four

16
00:00:44,014 --> 00:00:46,653
types that the immune system treats as different.

17
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:50,146
Incidence is the number of new cases in

18
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:52,266
a set time, for a set number of

19
00:00:52,347 --> 00:00:55,706
people, like cases per hundred thousand people per

20
00:00:55,867 --> 00:00:56,186
year.

21
00:00:56,827 --> 00:00:59,306
Prevalence is the share of a population that

22
00:00:59,387 --> 00:01:01,466
has the disease at one moment.

23
00:01:01,787 --> 00:01:02,906
Picture a tank.

24
00:01:03,147 --> 00:01:05,306
New cases flow in from a tap.

25
00:01:05,547 --> 00:01:08,106
Recovery or death drains it.

26
00:01:08,187 --> 00:01:10,586
Prevalence is how full the tank is.

27
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:14,760
Dengue illness lasts about a week, so few

28
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,000
people have it at any moment, even in

29
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:17,960
a big outbreak.

30
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:20,680
Past infection is a separate measure.

31
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:24,360
In a 2014 survey of Indonesian cities, about

32
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,640
seven in ten children had dengue antibodies, a

33
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:29,080
sign of past infection.

34
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:33,240
And to compare places, use rates, not counts:

35
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:36,200
divide the cases by the population, then multiply

36
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:37,880
by a hundred thousand.

37
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:39,880
A city of a million with two thousand

38
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,440
cases in a year has two hundred per

39
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:43,720
hundred thousand.

40
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,800
A town of fifty thousand with three hundred

41
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,640
cases has six hundred.

42
00:01:48,867 --> 00:01:51,826
Now read one city's weekly dengue incidence across

43
00:01:51,907 --> 00:01:52,666
a year.

44
00:01:52,947 --> 00:01:55,506
Cases stay low through the dry months, then

45
00:01:55,587 --> 00:01:57,266
climb a month or two after the wet

46
00:01:57,347 --> 00:01:58,226
season starts.

47
00:01:58,787 --> 00:01:59,906
Why the delay?

48
00:02:00,227 --> 00:02:04,066
Rain fills containers, eggs hatch, adults emerge, and

49
00:02:04,147 --> 00:02:07,186
the virus spends over a week multiplying inside

50
00:02:07,267 --> 00:02:10,026
each mosquito before it can be passed on.

51
00:02:10,307 --> 00:02:12,226
So the peak trails the rain.

52
00:02:13,434 --> 00:02:15,513
Here's where serotypes matter.

53
00:02:15,834 --> 00:02:18,713
You met herd immunity earlier: when enough people

54
00:02:18,754 --> 00:02:21,113
are immune, outbreaks fizzle.

55
00:02:21,234 --> 00:02:23,993
But dengue immunity covers only the serotype that

56
00:02:24,074 --> 00:02:25,433
infected you.

57
00:02:25,594 --> 00:02:27,433
So a city where most people have had

58
00:02:27,514 --> 00:02:30,073
dengue can still have a big outbreak when

59
00:02:30,154 --> 00:02:32,393
a different serotype arrives.

60
00:02:32,474 --> 00:02:35,033
A second infection with a new serotype is

61
00:02:35,234 --> 00:02:37,433
also more likely to be severe.

62
00:02:37,514 --> 00:02:40,073
So a dengue vaccine has to protect against

63
00:02:40,154 --> 00:02:42,793
all four types at once, or a type

64
00:02:42,874 --> 00:02:45,673
it misses could later cause severe dengue.

65
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:49,020
Mobility links the scales.

66
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:51,700
A person infected in one city can fly

67
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:54,020
to another within a day, while the virus

68
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:55,700
is still in their blood.

69
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:58,260
If local Aedes bite them, a new chain

70
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,140
starts.

71
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:02,660
That's how outbreaks in north Queensland usually begin,

72
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:04,900
with a traveller bringing the virus in.

73
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:09,140
So dengue spreads when four things meet: the

74
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:13,220
virus, Aedes mosquitoes, warmth and water to breed

75
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:16,420
in, and people who aren't immune, in contact.

76
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:19,700
Cut one far enough and the chain breaks.

77
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:22,940
And how people live shapes these conditions.

78
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:26,500
Where piped water is unreliable, families store water

79
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:29,140
in jars and tanks, and Aedes breeds in

80
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:31,060
them, even in dry months.

81
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:34,820
Crowded, fast-growing cities put more people near more

82
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:36,180
mosquitoes.

83
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:39,380
And control programs need money and support, year

84
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:40,340
after year.

85
00:03:41,567 --> 00:03:43,526
Now the Wolbachia evidence.

86
00:03:43,807 --> 00:03:47,886
In Yogyakarta, researchers randomly chose which of twenty-four

87
00:03:47,967 --> 00:03:50,606
areas got Wolbachia mosquitoes.

88
00:03:50,767 --> 00:03:52,366
Among people who came to clinics with a

89
00:03:52,447 --> 00:03:55,166
fever, about two in a hundred had dengue

90
00:03:55,247 --> 00:03:58,006
in Wolbachia areas, against about nine in a

91
00:03:58,047 --> 00:03:59,646
hundred elsewhere.

92
00:03:59,807 --> 00:04:02,366
Because the areas were chosen at random, weather

93
00:04:02,407 --> 00:04:05,486
and serotypes affected both groups alike.

94
00:04:05,567 --> 00:04:09,006
In Cairns, dengue incidence fell about ninety-six per

95
00:04:09,127 --> 00:04:10,846
cent after releases.

96
00:04:10,887 --> 00:04:12,926
But with no untreated areas to compare at

97
00:04:13,007 --> 00:04:15,886
the same time, other changes could explain part

98
00:04:15,927 --> 00:04:18,606
of that fall, so it's weaker evidence.

99
00:04:19,834 --> 00:04:22,553
So why hasn't Wolbachia stopped dengue?

100
00:04:23,034 --> 00:04:25,833
Releases take years and money to cover a

101
00:04:25,914 --> 00:04:28,313
city, and residents must agree to them.

102
00:04:28,794 --> 00:04:31,193
Many cities with dengue haven't started yet.

103
00:04:31,434 --> 00:04:35,513
Meanwhile, four serotypes make vaccines hard, many Aedes

104
00:04:35,594 --> 00:04:39,593
populations now resist common insecticides, a warmer climate

105
00:04:39,674 --> 00:04:41,993
may let Aedes spread to new places, and

106
00:04:42,074 --> 00:04:44,313
travel keeps bringing the virus back.

107
00:04:44,554 --> 00:04:47,193
Control succeeds where a method breaks a needed

108
00:04:47,274 --> 00:04:49,593
link, and people can keep it going.

109
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:52,200
Two lines to keep.

110
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:56,160
Incidence is the tap, prevalence is the tank.

111
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:00,640
And dengue needs virus, vector, warmth and water,

112
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:02,720
and people, all at once.

113
00:05:03,934 --> 00:05:05,693
Two exam questions.

114
00:05:06,014 --> 00:05:06,493
First.

115
00:05:07,134 --> 00:05:10,893
In 2023, District P, with two hundred thousand

116
00:05:11,054 --> 00:05:14,093
people, recorded one thousand dengue cases.

117
00:05:14,654 --> 00:05:18,173
District Q, with forty thousand people, recorded four

118
00:05:18,254 --> 00:05:19,213
hundred.

119
00:05:19,454 --> 00:05:22,093
Calculate the incidence in each district per hundred

120
00:05:22,254 --> 00:05:25,213
thousand people, and identify which district had the

121
00:05:25,414 --> 00:05:27,133
greater risk of dengue.

122
00:05:27,294 --> 00:05:28,493
Pause here to answer.

123
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:42,340
District P's incidence is one thousand, divided by

124
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:46,100
two hundred thousand, times a hundred thousand: five

125
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:47,860
hundred per hundred thousand.

126
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:52,020
District Q's is one thousand per hundred thousand.

127
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,500
So Q had double the risk, even with

128
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:57,300
fewer cases, because its cases come from a

129
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:58,500
smaller population.

130
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:01,140
Saying P is worse because it had more

131
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:03,460
cases is the classic mistake.

132
00:06:04,667 --> 00:06:05,226
Second.

133
00:06:05,707 --> 00:06:08,506
In City R, a survey found that eighty

134
00:06:08,587 --> 00:06:10,666
per cent of adults had antibodies from a

135
00:06:10,747 --> 00:06:14,586
past dengue infection, almost all from serotype one.

136
00:06:15,307 --> 00:06:17,946
Piped water is often cut off, so most

137
00:06:18,027 --> 00:06:20,626
homes store water in open jars.

138
00:06:20,987 --> 00:06:24,986
In 2025, travellers brought in serotype three, and

139
00:06:25,067 --> 00:06:27,466
the city had its largest dengue outbreak in

140
00:06:27,547 --> 00:06:30,666
ten years, with more severe cases than usual.

141
00:06:31,387 --> 00:06:35,306
Explain this outbreak, using biological and social factors.

142
00:06:35,867 --> 00:06:37,146
Pause here to answer.

143
00:06:47,567 --> 00:06:51,086
Immunity to serotype one doesn't protect against serotype

144
00:06:51,167 --> 00:06:54,446
three, so most people were still susceptible.

145
00:06:54,767 --> 00:06:57,646
Travellers brought the new serotype in, and Aedes

146
00:06:57,727 --> 00:07:00,446
breeding in the stored water passed it on.

147
00:07:00,567 --> 00:07:03,166
A second infection with a different serotype is

148
00:07:03,247 --> 00:07:06,126
more likely to be severe, so more cases

149
00:07:06,207 --> 00:07:07,486
were severe.

150
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:09,726
Saying eighty per cent were immune, so there

151
00:07:09,807 --> 00:07:12,606
couldn't be an outbreak, is the classic mistake.

152
00:07:13,834 --> 00:07:16,713
So for any disease data, check the measure

153
00:07:16,794 --> 00:07:19,753
and its population, then link each change to

154
00:07:19,834 --> 00:07:22,393
a biological or a social cause.

155
00:07:22,714 --> 00:07:26,793
Next, practise reading outbreak data and choosing controls.

156
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,440
Here are the key notes.

157
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:31,600
Pause the video if you need more time

158
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:32,480
to copy them.
